description:
memory.rs:828-840 (set_default_calendar) and :857-871 (set_default_participant_identity) each construct a SetDefaultResult via Default + field-assign:

  let mut result = SetDefaultResult::default();
  if !known {
      return Ok(result);
  }

  let previous = inner.aux_ref(account_id.as_ref()).and_then(|a| a.default_calendar.clone());
  inner.aux_mut(account_id.as_ref()).default_calendar = Some(calendar_id.clone());

  result.new_default = Some(calendar_id.clone());
  result.previous_default = previous;
  Ok(result)

SetDefaultResult is #[non_exhaustive], but the attribute only forbids struct-literal *from other crates*. memory.rs is in the same crate as backend.rs::SetDefaultResult, so struct-literal IS allowed and would be cleaner:

  if !known {
      return Ok(SetDefaultResult::default());
  }

  let previous = inner.aux_ref(account_id.as_ref()).and_then(|a| a.default_calendar.clone());
  inner.aux_mut(account_id.as_ref()).default_calendar = Some(calendar_id.clone());

  Ok(SetDefaultResult {
      new_default: Some(calendar_id.clone()),
      previous_default: previous,
  })

The MockBackend in lib.rs:828 and :857 actually does use struct-literal syntax — so the pattern is inconsistent within the same crate. The struct-literal form has two real advantages:
1. It is exhaustive: if SetDefaultResult ever gains a field, the compiler errors at the construction site, forcing the author to think about the new field. The mut-and-assign form silently uses Default for any new field.
2. It is a single expression returning the value, not three separate assignments. Code-flow reads as 'compute these values, return them' instead of 'allocate empty, fill in, return'.

The 'mut result + assign + return' form is what you'd write from outside the crate where struct-literal is blocked. Inside the crate, the canonical idiom is struct-literal.
